A||||12||CUTTER||HORSEPOWER!!!!
Z||000000||CUTTER||08-26-99||01:34 AM||||I need more horsepower in my 87GT, but not alot of money, already have a flowmaster, need to get a K&N airfilter, any other suggestions on what would make a decent horsepower increase but wont cost alot of the green stuff? looking to spend under 100 dollars (US) and want somewhere from 15-20 horsepower increase......help!!  those fords are getting to me bad!!!! (rustangs)
Z||000001||batboy||08-26-99||05:58 AM||||Headers and a free-flowing exhaust system will give you that much extra horsepower, but it'll cost you more than your budget. I can't think of anything that will increase that much power for only $100, better save up some more cash.
Z||000002||Taijiguy||08-26-99||04:53 PM||||Start off by saving yourself the bucks on a K&N filter setup, (Unless you just like the idea of permanant filter, I read they don't really increase performance) That's not from personal experience, just what I've read here. If you want performance on a budget, be ready for some labor. Buy yourself a new set of topside gaskets, and gasket match the intake and plenums, and port the heads and exhaust manifold. Those mods will help out the flow of air and exhaust. Maybe iinvest in a set of Splitfire, or Bosch platimun tips, a high energy coil, and 9 mm wires. Maybe a high isolation cap and rotor set. Short of a ADS performance chip, those are about as cheap as you can get in the performance boost department. Toss in a can of Octane Plus, and you should be pushin' around 500 horse......<p>(OK, maybe not quite 500........that's just the wiseass in me getting loose.)  Sorry.
Z||000003||Eric||08-26-99||07:45 PM||||How about welding the flages on the exaust manifolds and grinding out the restrictions.  Should give about eight ponies but I've heard it's really difficult without dropping the engine.  If you can manage to do that, take care of the intake porting too and you would be looking at a 20 horsepower increase for just a little bit of money.
Z||000004||batboy||08-26-99||08:08 PM||||Hey, there you go Cutter. Sounds like the boys gave you a pretty decent budget plan to get started. Since I don't have a V6 Fiero, I keep forgetting the stock ignition system needs upgraded in order to get decent performance in the higher rpm range.
Z||000005||WisconsinGT||08-26-99||08:50 PM||||Cutter, I am picking up my baby (88GT) tomorrow. What I had my mechanic do is the following... New heads (Wanted to pocket port them, but would void warranty), Port and weld stock exhaust (No need to drop engine.), new dis. cap and rotor, new plugs and wires, 180 stat. Not much, but I just want a good, reliable, non gas guzzling ride. I will let you know how it feels.
Z||000006||lowCG||08-26-99||08:58 PM||||Tried that "cheap horsepower with no extras" concept once myself;got a set of grinder bits,and ported away,carefully tapering the intake from the huge (slide valve,variable venturi)carburetors on the custom intake I'd made from tubing.A solid lifter cam was an added expense,but very beneficial.<br>Ran great from 3500 rpm on up,no bottom end at all.<br>Totall cost was$60,for gaskets,grinding tools,welding.<br>On a Fiero,you could use a 290DEG cam,carburetor(with vacume secondaies),Edelbrock intake,and some serious,careful porting and polishing.<br>A lot of tuning on the carb will assure a tolerable low rpm range,and establish a good power curve.If you screw up the port job,you'll actually lose horsepower,and gain a much better understanding of why Porsches cost as much as they do.
Z||000007||Shaun41178||08-26-99||10:08 PM||||I suggest grinding out the restrictions(bits will only cost $15 or so and that will give an 6-10 hp increase and improve throttle response, and then get a power pulley for $80.  There that is under $100.  If you don't mind having the car down for a while I would gasket mathc the intake and heads as well.I got my bits at an Ace Hardware store<p>Engine does not have to be dropped to get the manifolds out of the engine compartment.<p>If you save up another $130 or less(depending on what kind you get), you can install 1.6 rockers while the intake and exhasut are off and access to the valve covers will be easier.  This mod will give about 7-10 hp in the upper rpm range and perhaps more with ported manifolds and gasket matched intake and heads.  With the gasket matching, 1.6 rockers and underdrive pulley you should pull that mustang that outran you the other night.  I am guessing it was your friends car.?<br>'<br>Good luck
Z||000008||88 Fiero||08-29-99||01:36 PM||||Cutter...is your 87 GT a automatic or a stick shift...If it's automatic...you can convert that to the getrag 5-speed. you want horsepower...hook up with a set of headers...FOCOA has one for $550.00 a set. This headers are tune and dynoed up to 15 horsepower. The guy who designed this headers is the same guy who makes headers for the Buick Grand National Turbo V-6...
Z||000009||CUTTER||08-29-99||08:01 PM||||My car is auto. Im thinking of just geting a 350 small block and puting that in instead of puting headers on.
Z||000010||lowCG||08-29-99||11:33 PM||||I tend to agree,and am not going to do anything costly to my stock V6 either ;it's hard for me to justify putting money into something that'll need a rebuild(140,000 miles on it now),and is performance limited by it's design.<br>I'm determined to find a,low mile,4 cam V6(3400)for $1,500,and spend a little time putting it in.
Z||000011||terryk||08-30-99||12:32 PM||||If you port the heads, leave the intake vane alone (bump). That is what increased the head flow 17% over the stock LE7 heads.
Z||000012||terryk||08-30-99||02:05 PM||||Oops, the vane improved the LH7 head's flow 17% over the stock LE2 heads (not LE7).
